WEBVTT

00:00:00.214 --> 00:00:01.103
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Part 2

00:00:02.278 --> 00:00:05.833
<v><b>Emma Barnett:</b></v> You’re listening to the story of Anthony Ray Hinton.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.419
He spent three decades in prison for a crime he didn’t commit,

00:00:09.419 --> 00:00:11.062
but he didn’t give up,

00:00:11.435 --> 00:00:15.399
and eventually his case was taken up by the Equal Justice Initiative,

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:19.529
a group in Alabama that fights racism in the US justice system.

00:00:19.877 --> 00:00:22.452
<v><b>Anthony Ray Hinton:</b></v> What gave me hope, more than anything,

00:00:22.485 --> 00:00:26.949
was when I met Bryan Stevenson from the Equal Justice Initiative,

00:00:27.084 --> 00:00:31.598
and I saw that his skin was the same colour as my skin,

00:00:32.027 --> 00:00:37.000
and I felt for the first time that he being an African-American,

00:00:37.025 --> 00:00:41.672
he would do everything in his earthly power to try and prove my innocence.

00:00:42.172 --> 00:00:50.165
And I was right about that. He, along with the lawyers and the EJI staff,

00:00:50.165 --> 00:00:56.569
they worked for sixteen long years to win my freedom.

00:00:56.886 --> 00:01:02.948
Sixteen long years that they had to fight back and forth in the courts,

00:01:03.297 --> 00:01:08.358
arguing that this was wrong, and finally we won our –

00:01:08.406 --> 00:01:12.149
my – freedom, April the 3rd, 2015.

00:01:12.648 --> 00:01:14.319
<v><b>Emma Barnett:</b></v> What was that moment like?

00:01:14.604 --> 00:01:16.505
<v><b>Anthony Ray Hinton:</b></v> Well, I never will forget…

00:01:16.505 --> 00:01:21.770
I got up and I called Byan Stevenson and he told me,

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:27.942
he said, ‘Ray, you going home.’ And I hadn’t heard that word, ‘home’, in 30 years.

00:01:28.268 --> 00:01:33.932
And I sat on the floor and I cried like a baby.

00:01:34.559 --> 00:01:41.148
And all the young men that was around me saw that I was crying,

00:01:41.148 --> 00:01:42.881
and they, probably, at the moment,

00:01:43.189 --> 00:01:46.551
they probably thought that I had got some bad news.

00:01:46.583 --> 00:01:50.658
And I’ll never forget, one of the young guys, he came up,

00:01:50.658 --> 00:01:54.705
he said, ‘Pop, something wrong?’ And I looked up at him

00:01:54.705 --> 00:01:57.139
and I said, er, ‘I’m going home.’

00:01:57.448 --> 00:02:02.138
And they told me how proud they was that I was going home,

00:02:02.265 --> 00:02:04.913
and even some gave me advice, and:

00:02:05.337 --> 00:02:09.949
‘Pop, it’s a different world out there than what you was used to.’

00:02:10.456 --> 00:02:15.421
Me being locked up 30 years, and the world had passed me by,

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:24.120
and I got my very first taste of modern technology when I got in my best friend’s car – Lester.

00:02:25.318 --> 00:02:29.635
He asked me, when they released me, ‘Where do you wanna go first?’

00:02:30.207 --> 00:02:35.242
and I said, ‘I want you to take me where they laid my mother’s body.’

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:38.185
<v><b>Emma Barnett:</b></v> Because your mother died while you were in prison?

00:02:38.255 --> 00:02:40.871
<v><b>Anthony Ray Hinton:</b></v> Yes. And he said, ‘OK.’

00:02:41.156 --> 00:02:47.385
And I see him fidgeting with – what I thought he was trying to find

00:02:47.384 --> 00:02:50.856
– a radio station so I could hear some good music.

00:02:51.095 --> 00:02:55.520
And as we got on the road, I hear this white lady voice.

00:02:56.073 --> 00:03:01.627
She said, ‘In one tenth of a mile, turn right.’ And it frightened me!

00:03:01.652 --> 00:03:06.016
I said, ‘What the hell?’ And I looks over at him,

00:03:06.437 --> 00:03:09.091
he’s laughing and he’s looking at how hysterical

00:03:09.091 --> 00:03:13.770
I must have been looking and I was too afraid to look back.

00:03:14.188 --> 00:03:20.548
He veer off to the right and he said, ‘That’s no white lady back there,’ he said.

00:03:20.548 --> 00:03:24.940
‘This is GPS, I told you everything have changed,’ he said.

00:03:25.734 --> 00:03:35.508
‘She gonna tell me exactly how to get to the cemetery.’ He said, ‘This is modern technology.’

00:03:35.563 --> 00:03:40.759
I said, ‘You telling me that we don’t have to stop at the filling station

00:03:40.759 --> 00:03:42.759
and ask for directions no more?’

00:03:43.609 --> 00:03:49.576
He said, ‘Yes, I told you that you gonna have to learn everything over again.’

00:03:50.036 --> 00:03:54.568
<v><b>Emma Barnett:</b></v> Do you feel pleasure? Are you able to enjoy your life?

00:03:54.593 --> 00:03:56.418
Do you believe in your freedom?

00:03:56.958 --> 00:04:00.398
<v><b>Anthony Ray Hinton:</b></v> I believe in my freedom, I enjoy life every day.

00:04:00.621 --> 00:04:03.583
I enjoy life more than anything.

00:04:03.662 --> 00:04:09.463
I realize that I took my life and my freedom for granted,

00:04:09.819 --> 00:04:14.301
and it wasn’t until I got locked up, and I realized that

00:04:14.302 --> 00:04:18.233
there is nothing in this world more important than your freedom…

00:04:18.796 --> 00:04:25.194
and I promised myself that I would enjoy my freedom until the day I die,

00:04:25.337 --> 00:04:29.881
after spending 30 years of pure hell in a cage.

00:04:30.286 --> 00:04:34.299
I don’t care when it raining, I walks in the rain because

00:04:34.324 --> 00:04:38.605
for 30 years rain was not allowed to fall on my body,

00:04:38.803 --> 00:04:45.192
and every time it rain I walks in the rain, it feel, like, so cool and so refreshing.

00:04:46.335 --> 00:04:52.135
Freedom is the most beautiful thing any man or woman can have.